        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T10:00:31.014Z",
        "body": "**Combined pre-merge review verdict (2026-08-13, two independent passes: decision-coherence audit + fresh-eyes correctness).**\n\n**Code: sound.** All 13 decided properties HOLD with test pins (server-owned ladders, do-nothing disposition, observe-only breaker with shared claim budget, piece-1-pays-nothing, exported pure validation, tail-repeat arithmetic, three-numbers cross-check, non-goal docs, unlimited MaxDeliver, natsmsgtest demotion). Lifecycle joins, budget races, capture ordering, arithmetic edges all verified clean. Note: the unit suite already runs Start/Run/poll/ladder tests against an embedded real broker — not purely fake-based.\n\n**One CONFIRMED bug to fix on the branch (medium-high, pre-existing but newly load-bearing):** `natsmsg/deadletter.go:84-88` copies **all** original headers into the DLQ publish — a message carrying `Nats-Expected-Stream` (or `Nats-Expected-Last-*`, `Nats-Rollup`) can **never be captured** (reproduced: err 10060 on every attempt → full ladder → `OutcomeStranded`, floor pinned, breaker equally defeated). One real producer class defeats the \"never drop, never strand silently\" guarantee. Fix: strip JetStream control headers on copy (keep `Nats-Msg-Id` — its dedupe of re-capture-after-failed-Ack is desirable; make that deliberate) + a regression test.\n\n**One cheap hardening:** the terminate path runs capture+DoubleAck on the handler ctx, which is already cancelled during documented shutdown ordering (cancel → join → drain) while the connection is still up — a shutdown racing the *final* delivery manufactures a strand by pure timing. `context.WithoutCancel` (keeping the 15s bound) on the terminate path.\n\n**Doc/comment sweep (blocking per the coherence audit; no behavior changes):**\n- retry_test.go:321 and 1015-17 — comments assert \"Term does not settle\"; the suite proved Term settles.\n- retry_test.go:292-94 — remove the irreproducible floor-2/blocker-6 figures.\n- retry_test.go:263, 823-25, 849-51+871, 882-84 — Nak-era comments contradicting their own tests' assertions.\n- jsconsumer.go:26-28 — package doc overclaims \"dead-lettering the message pinning a stalled floor\"; align with retry.go:377-383's disclaimer.\n- schedule.go:203-05 — violation text teaches the disproved model (server silently *normalizes* AckWait to BackOff[0] on the ordinary path).\n- schedule_test.go:30-31 — \"flat client ladder … the shape Track A actually ships\" superseded.\n- retry.go:527 — \"run NewRetry locally\" advice misattributed (ladder checks live in Config.validate/Schedule).\n- retry.go:600-02 — duplicated RecoverBy comparison; call the shared Schedule check instead.\n- README — add the measured-clock restart trade-off (\"late, never early\") to the Non-goal section.\n- Cosmetic: schedule.go:199 \"%d redeliveries\" prints MaxDeliver (n deliveries = n−1 redeliveries); retry_test.go:223/227 comment names the wrong test.\n\n**Post-merge follow-ups noted, non-blocking:** observe-mode trip counts overcount vs enforce for a true blocker (doc line); double-Start/standalone-Retry misuse is silent (log line); R3/clustered + interest-retention behaviors flagged for brokersemantics/staging verification.\n\n**Verdict: merge after this one fix pass** (ordinary push, history append — no rewrite needed)."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T13:24:00.028Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T13:23:59.973Z",
        "body": "**A1 final state (2026-08-12): green and awaiting commit go.** Client-ladder execution path deleted (`RetryConfig` has no ladder fields — mixed ownership is now a *type error*, not a runtime check); 72 tests, full gate clean; the ENT-1535 production config regression test still proves both original defects are caught from config alone. Uncommitted pending Andrea's go in the implementation session.\n\nTwo adoption-guidance refinements from the final shape:\n- The **FloorAge &gt; 15m** rule applies only to consumers attaching the monitor for the **observe soak** (entire-search is one) — for plain adopters the ladder-vs-`MaxTimeToDeadLetter` bound is what gates the SLA; RecoverBy-vs-FloorAge is only checked when a Monitor is attached.\n- **Three numbers must agree** for a consumer: the consumer's `MaxDeliver`, `len(BackOff)` semantics (tail-repeat — see the counting rule on COR-1255), and the Retry's `MaxDeliver`. `Start` cross-checks and fails loudly on mismatch; adoption PRs should expect that error rather than being surprised by it."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T13:16:17.765Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T13:16:17.722Z",
        "body": "**Architecture decision (2026-08-12, Andrea): the CR/server owns the redelivery ladder — option (b).**\n\nThe exported validation surfaced a fork that could not stay unresolved: a library-owned ladder (`NakWithDelay`) and a CR-carried server `backOff` fighting each other is precisely the ENT-1535 defect, so exactly one may own redelivery per consumer. Decision: **server/CR owns it.** Apps plain-Nak; go-nuts `Retry` keeps capture-then-Ack, telemetry, and the observe soak; the client-ladder execution path (zero adopters) is deleted or marked for deletion. `Schedule.Validate` keeps modeling both modes so the *mixed* case stays detectable forever — that check is the ENT-1535 defect detector.\n\nWhy (b): ladder changes become fleet PRs with no app deploy; merge-time validation covers the *actual* schedule (under (a) the real timing lives in app config, invisible to fleet CI); bind-only mode becomes natural (client expectations validated against the durable's real server ladder at startup); consistent with the streams \"Option A\" philosophy.\n\n**Adoption guidance update (supersedes earlier bullets):** entire-search adopts via its existing CreateOrUpdate setting server-side `BackOff` (bounded, per `MaxTimeToDeadLetter` arithmetic) + plain Nak + Retry for capture/telemetry/observe (FloorAge 20m per the documented example — three flat 5m rungs hit exactly 15m and the ≥ check rejects). When its Consumer CR lands under Track D, the CR full-mirrors a server config that already matches — clean adoption, then bind-only. No second migration."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T13:06:48.830Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T13:06:48.777Z",
        "body": "**Scope change (2026-08-12, decided by Andrea after review churn) — split and demote; supersedes the \"set `BreakerEnforce` explicitly\" line in the handoff notes above.**\n\nRepeated major-bug discoveries in review of the *enforce* path were a state-space signal: acting on client-side inference about which message blocks the floor is where every big bug lived. Meanwhile the simple design is production-validated (entire-search #173: bounded ~43 m ladder + exhaustion capture, five clean days), and the strongest pro-breaker argument — \"ladders lie\" — is resolved at the root by the schedule unification in this same work (schedules lied because app `NakWithDelay` fought config BackOff; the library now owns one schedule).\n\nNew shape:\n1. **Ships now (carries M1):** unified library-owned retry schedule + full construction validation (RecoverBy, largest-rung, and a new max-cumulative-ladder bound) + exhaustion capture-then-Ack + **FloorMonitor as telemetry only** (metric + page, no action). M1 is met by adopters configuring validated bounded ladders (≤ ~25 m cumulative).\n2. **Soaks:** the breaker in `BreakerObserve` mode only; the enforce path stays in code with its tests but is documented experimental / not-for-production pending a multi-week soak of real trip counts. **Deletion is an explicitly acceptable outcome** if the soak shows bounded ladders suffice.\n3. **Effort redirect:** mass-transient selectivity mattered mostly because DLQ replay is manual (deep-dive risk #7) — replay automation to be scoped as follow-up to COR-1256.\n\nentire-search adoption accordingly: bounded ladder + observe mode."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T12:50:33.167Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T12:50:33.133Z",
        "body": "**Handoff notes for the next two steps** (from the A1 implementation session, 2026-08-12):\n\n*entire-search adoption PR (after the go-nuts merge):*\n- Set `Breaker: BreakerEnforce` **explicitly** — the zero value observes.\n- `RecoverBy: 4`, per the measured recovery envelope (ENT-1535 forensics: recoveries exist at attempt 4, none at ≥5).\n- **Arithmetic trap:** with the documented MaxDeliver 6 / flat 5m ladder, cumulative wait to RecoverBy 4 is exactly 15m — which trips the strict construction check. FloorAge must exceed 15m, or use a 4m rung. Run `NewRetry` once locally before wiring the PR; the validation is deliberately strict and easy to get wrong by one rung.\n\n*A5 poison-conformance canary:*\n- Assert the stall SLA as **`FloorAge + largest rung + AckWait` computed from each consumer's actual shipped config** — not a hardcoded 30m. The two drift apart the moment someone tunes a ladder.\n\n*Status:* review-ready; full gate clean. Shape: `jsconsumer/floor.go` (FloorMonitor, ~310 loc) + `jsconsumer/retry.go` (~940) + tests (~1,480) new; `jsconsumer.go`, `natsmsgtest/fakemsg.go`, README, doc.go modified. Uncommitted on the working tree pending explicit go-ahead — no branch yet."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T12:44:57.428Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T12:50:28.444Z",
        "body": "**A1 implementation status — settled design decisions (2026-08-12, pre-merge):** breaker implemented in go-nuts `jsconsumer`; 62 tests, full gate clean (gofmt -s, build, vet, test, -race, golangci-lint 0 issues); uncommitted pending go-ahead.\n\nDecisions of record:\n1. **Trigger measures elapsed failure time, not delivery count** — `NumDelivered` inflates on AckWait expiry (panic/kill/eviction): four deliveries can land in 68 ms with zero rungs served, so count-based inference over-estimates exactly when it matters. See the correction thread on ENT-1535.\n2. **`RetryConfig.RecoverBy`** — the adopter declares the delivery by which a transient is expected to clear; construction rejects any ladder whose cumulative wait to RecoverBy reaches the monitor's FloorAge (a separate largest-rung check also kept). The upper bound against the stall SLA stays the adopter's arithmetic (`FloorAge + largest rung + AckWait`) — the library cannot know the SLA.\n3. **Selectivity under mass failure:** at most **one claimed message per stall window**, window resets only when the ack floor *moves* — successive floor-holders drain back-to-back, a claim that didn't move the floor waits out a full window, and a false positive is bounded to one message per FloorAge. Verified by a 200-failing-messages test (exactly 1 dead-lettered per window) and a three-successive-floor-holders test.\n4. **Trigger behind an internal `stallSignal` interface** (FloorMonitor separate from Retry) — a future native MaxPendingAge-style server trigger slots in without touching the capture-then-Ack path.\n5. **`BreakerObserve` consumes the same one-per-stall-window budget as enforcing** — observe mode is a faithful dry run: the trip counts a soak collects are exactly what enforce would produce, not the far larger number of messages that merely satisfied the conditions. Without this, soaks would overestimate the enforce rate and nobody would ever flip the switch. Documented on the constant; tested (50 further qualifying messages in the same window → zero additional trips)."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T12:47:47.115Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T12:47:47.096Z",
        "body": "**Breaker non-goal (recorded 2026-08-12)** — two distinct cases; only the second is attributable to the measured-clock design decision:\n\n1. **A handler that dies on the poison message (panic/OOM) rather than returning an error.** `Settle` is the only entry point to the library's dispositions — ladder, exhaustion, and breaker alike — so such a message is not settled by *any* of them, under any trigger design; this is a property of the callback contract, not of the elapsed-time clock. **Detection is unaffected:** FloorMonitor polls independently of message flow and keeps reporting the stall for as long as the process lives; the ack-floor monitor still pages; a crash-looping pod is its own alerting surface. **Remediation is deliberately not attempted:** recovering the panic in `Process` to Ack/dead-letter the message is rejected as contrary to the module's fatal-panic posture (`nuts.ShutdownGroup` recovers a panicking loop only to drain, then treats it as fatal to the group — \"so a dead consumer cannot leave its process looking healthy\"). A handler that can't survive its input needs the crash fixed, not the evidence Acked away. Any future change here should be a conscious reversal of that module-wide stance, not a quiet addition to the breaker.\n\n2. **Measured-clock trade-off (the actual design consequence):** a message failing *normally* in a process that restarts for unrelated reasons (rollout, unrelated OOM) loses its accumulated stall window — the breaker fires late, never early. Safe direction, consistent with FloorMonitor's existing conservatism.\n\nBoth documented in the Retry package doc (\"Non-goal\" section) and README."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T11:09:20.457Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T11:51:55.291Z",
        "body": "**Design rationale — \"why not just shorten the backoff ladder?\"** (asked during revie…2559 tokens truncated…mer CRs exist in any cluster.\n\n**Field-manual corrections (verified against installed CRD / nack 0.23.0 / jsm.go 0.4.1 / nats-server 2.14.4):**\n- The field is **`spec.backoff`, all-lowercase** — `backOff:` is *silently pruned* by the API server; `kubeconform -strict` is the only guard.\n- Adoption-merge has three field classes: CRD-defaulted (always sent), unconditionally-sent-as-zero, sent-only-if-set (omission retains). Danger is not uniform: `ackPolicy`/`deliverPolicy`/`replayPolicy`/`maxWaiting` are immutable-on-update and **fail loudly, durable untouched**; **`ackWait` is the sole silent catastrophe** (mutable, CRD default 1ns).\n- nack runs pedantic **unconditionally** (hardcoded), so `ackWait == backoff[0]` is server-enforced.\n- `preventDelete` protects the durable, not its config; reverting a CR change does not un-apply it.\n\n**Two structural blockers for CR adoption beyond the canary:**\n1. **CR-owned ladders are ordering-blocked on bind-only:** jsconsumer `Start` always writes `BackOff`, so an app with a nil ladder *clears* a CR-owned ladder on every start and nack restores it ~1 min later — permanent config flapping. Bind-only mode (D0, not yet built) must exist before any ladder-carrying CR.\n2. **mirror-pipeline durables are not adoptable:** every fanout loop (a) relies on server-side `InactiveThreshold` reaping (24–72h) as the safety valve from the 2026-06-01 interest-stream incident — a Consumer CR defeats it, nack recreating the reaped durable within ~1 min and restoring the interest pin permanently; and (b) schedules redelivery client-side (NakWithDelay 30s×7), so a CR would mirror server config while the real timing contract lives in the app, invisible to the E1 lint. Loops must migrate to jsconsumer + server ladders FIRST; CRs, if ever, come after.\n\nSide-finding to triage separately: `meta-fanout-backfill` (github_meta_backfill) has a `/readyz` coupling that fails latently at the next rollout.\n\n**Sequencing consequence regardless of park/proceed: the original \"CR + bind-only per loop in one change\" recipe inverts — library migration first, CRs second.** Parking decision + branch preservation pending Andrea."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T13:16:22.207Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T13:16:22.169Z",
        "body": "**Ladder-ownership decision affecting all Track D CRs (2026-08-12, Andrea): Consumer CRs carry the redelivery ladder (`backOff`) — the server/CR is the single owner; apps plain-Nak.** Full rationale on ENT-1601.\n\nConsequences for this track:\n- Full-mirror CRs include `backOff` (+ `ackWait = backoff[0]`, pedantic mode) — already implied by the field manual; now it is also *the* ladder, not a mirror of a client-side one.\n- Migrating apps drop any client-side redelivery scheduling as part of their jsconsumer migration; go-nuts `Retry` provides capture-then-Ack, telemetry, and the observe soak only.\n- Bind-only mode validates client-side expectations (`RecoverBy`, `FloorAge`, `MaxTimeToDeadLetter` arithmetic) against the durable's actual server ladder at startup — closed loop, no inference.\n- Fleet CI admission (COR-1255) validates the complete timing contract from the CR alone using go-nuts' exported `jsconsumer.Schedule.Validate` — including the mixed-ownership violation, which must never pass review.\n- The staging canary is unaffected (full-mirror already includes `backOff`); note the durable chosen should have a server-side ladder or none, and the CR mirrors reality either way."
      }
    ]
