        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T11:36:19.114Z",
        "body": "**Two E1 line items inherited from Track D's migration kickoff (2026-08-14):**\n\n1. **Fleet-side timing-envelope invariant:** the cmd/worker migration carries an app-side test pinning `MaxDeliver × syncTimeout + scheduled waits < mirror_git_v1 maxAge` (measured 5h04m vs 6h), but mirror-pipeline cannot import fleet's CR, so the maxAge is a mirrored named constant — the app test catches app-side erosion only. The fleet-side half belongs here: an admission check in fleet CI asserting `mirror_git_v1` maxAge stays ≥ the documented worker give-up envelope (pointer comments on both sides reference each other). Generalizes to any workqueue whose consumer's wall-clock envelope approaches its maxAge.\n\n2. **Tooling dependency for config-level checks:** the entire MCP NATS toolset exposes consumer *runtime state* only (floor, pending, redelivered) — not consumer *config* (AckWait, MaxDeliver, BackOff, InactiveThreshold, MaxAckPending, policies). Track D's per-PR stored-vs-rendered diffs are human-gated (`nats consumer info -j` by hand) until a consumer-config read exists; E1's admission/verification work will hit the same wall. Candidate small ticket: add consumer-config read to the MCP nats tools."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T13:19:07.499Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T13:23:55.627Z",
        "body": "**Ladder-arithmetic trap for the admission lint (2026-08-12, found during the A1 ladder-ownership cutover):** JetStream **repeats the last `backOff` rung** when the array is shorter than `maxDeliver` — a short array under a larger maxDeliver is the *common* CR shape, and summing the array as written under-counts real time-to-dead-letter, i.e. a naive checker passes ladders that breach the bound in production. Fixed in go-nuts' `Schedule.TimeToDeadLetter` with boundary tests.\n\n**Counting rule, stated precisely (an off-by-one lives here):** the repeat covers **rungs served before delivery N** — i.e. deliveries `1..DeadLetterDelivery−1`. Example: 3-rung array `[5m,10m,30m]`, MaxDeliver 7, capture reserve 1 → dead-letter at delivery 6 → sums **five** rungs (5+10+30+30+30 = 1h45m), not six. Say \"rungs served before delivery N\", never \"rungs up to N\".\n\nStanding rule for E1: the lint must call go-nuts' exported validation (`jsconsumer.Schedule`), never re-implement the arithmetic — this trap and its off-by-one are exactly why."
      },
      {
        "createdAt": "2026-08-12T13:09:35.314Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T13:09:35.285Z",
        "body": "**E1 admission-control mechanism, sharpened by the A1 scope change (2026-08-12):** with the enforcing breaker demoted to an observe soak (see ENT-1601), the M1 stall-safety property is now \"validated bounded ladders\" — and once Track D lands Consumer CRs in fleet, that property is **fully computable at merge time**: `backoff`, `maxDeliver`, `ackWait` all live in the CR, so the lint can compute cumulative ladder time per consumer per rendered cell overlay, plus cross-resource checks against the stream CR (ladder vs `maxAge` redelivery envelope, DLQ pairing).\n\nImplementation note to prevent drift: **do not re-implement the arithmetic**. go-nuts is exporting its schedule validation as a pure function (requested of the A1 session); the fleet CI checker should compile that same function over rendered CRs. One source of truth, three enforcement points: fleet CI refuses the merge (admission), NACK makes the server match the CR (generation), and bind-only jsconsumer refuses to start if its client-side expectations (`RecoverBy`/`FloorAge`) don't match the durable's actual server-side ladder (library). Client-side knobs stay app-config and are validated at bind time against real server state — no inference anywhere in the chain."
      }
    ]
  },
